text:
maria: Quick heads-up: minimal sample app fails to compile on edge with our JS SDK. probable overload/generic inference weirdness. anyone seen this?
jon: can you paste the exact error + small repro?
maria: yup, paste below 
```
src/main.ts:8:5 - error TS2769: No overload matches this call.
  Overload 1 of 2, '(route: string): Promise<string>', gave the following error.
    Argument of type '{ input: string; }' is not assignable to parameter of type 'string'.

// repro
import Redwood from "redwood-sdk";
const client = new Redwood.Client();

async function go() {
  const out = await client.generate<{ userId: string }>("summarize", { input: "hello" });
  // expected out -> { userId: string }
}
```
kevin: sounds like an import resolution + types entry mismatch. Are you bundling for the edge (esbuild/rollup) and targeting esnext vs nodenext?
sophia: TS config matters here. moduleResolution and module can cause TS to pick different declaration files. older projects with `module: "esnext"` can hit this.
maria: our example starter uses conservative tsconfig so customers copy it. we can't force nodenext everywhere.
devx-bot: docs: <https://internal.devx.redwood/edge-interop> note about exports map + typesEntries
sam: two quick workarounds: 1) call site explicit generic + cast: `client.generate<{ userId: string }>("summarize", { input: "hi" } as any)` or 2) change SDK typings to a single generic signature (avoid overloads).
kevin: another failure mode: consumers end up reading the CJS d.ts because `types` points to dist/cjs/index.d.ts. ensure package.json `types` points to ESM types and `exports` map is clear.
sophia: pragmatic SDK fix: add a fallback signature `generate<T = any>(route: string, opts?: unknown): Promise<T>` so weird tsconfigs keep working. it's not perfect but reduces friction.
maria: quick local patch that fixed our starter sample:
```
// in sdk types.d.ts
export function generate<T = any>(route: string, opts?: { input?: string }): Promise<T>;
```
jon: if we change SDK types, add changelog and call out potential breakages. also recommend `skipLibCheck: true` in sample tsconfig as a temporary mitigation.
sophia: we should add a tsd test matrix compiling with `module: "esnext"` and `module: "nodenext"` and a small edge bundle to catch regressions. i'll add it to sdk CI.
kevin: consider adding an `./edge` entry in `exports` pointing to a specific ESM build + types to keep edge consumers on the right bundle.
maria: will open an issue with a mini-repro repo and tag eng-runtime + devx, attaching failing sample + patch.
sam: side note: `unknown` vs `any` in the SDK signature can change inference behavior. making opts `unknown` forces callers to assert, which can avoid silent inference collapse, but it's breaking. use carefully.
maria: thanks all, i'll post the issue link once created :thumbsup:
